data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"researcher","content":"- CRISPR is a genetic editing tool adapted from a bacterial defense system: guide RNA (crRNA and tracrRNA) identifies target DNA, and a Cas9 complex cuts it [1][3].\n- Cas9 is delivered into a cell with a synthetic guide RNA, so the genome can be cut at a chosen site to remove or add genes [7].\n- Scientists use CRISPR to \"knock out\" genes, and can add repair templates to replace disease-causing mutations with healthy sequences [2].\n- Modified Cas9 can activate gene expression instead of cutting DNA, which helps researchers study gene function [3]."}

